  "id": "fokuspartikeln",
  "name": "Focus adverbs: only, even, also, too",
  "level": "B2",
  "subtitle": "Only, even and also go next to the word they are about, and the meaning changes with the position: Only Mia saw it. Mia saw only the film. Use also in the middle of a sentence, and too or as well at the end.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "Only",
    "even",
    "also",
    "too",
    "as well"
  ],
  "source": "EnglishAcademy original English text (grammar-b2 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "only / even / also / just",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "+ the word they are about",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "= that word is the focus"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-verb\">Only</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">Mia</span> saw the film (nobody else). — Mia saw <span class=\"vp-verb\">only</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">the film</span> (nothing else).",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>Focus adverbs</strong> (<em>only, even, also, just</em>) go directly before the word or phrase they are about. In speech we stress that word. Move the adverb, and the meaning changes.",
        "tag": "Focus"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Normal position:</strong> before the main verb, after <em>be</em>, and after the first auxiliary. <em>She also speaks French. / He is also a teacher. / They can also swim. / I have even tried it.</em>",
        "tag": "Position"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>only</strong> means <em>nobody / nothing else</em>. <em>Only Mia saw the film.</em> (Nobody else saw it.) <em>Mia saw only the film.</em> (She saw nothing else.) The safest place is directly before the word you mean. In speech, many people put <em>only</em> before the verb and stress the word they mean: <em>I only saw MIA.</em>",
        "tag": "only"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>even</strong> shows surprise: more than you expect. <em>Even Tom was late.</em> (Tom is never late.) <em>She even speaks Welsh.</em> With a negative, it means <em>not the smallest thing</em>: <em>He didn't even say hello.</em>",
        "tag": "even"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>also, too, as well.</strong> <em>Also</em> goes in the middle: <em>She also plays tennis.</em> <em>Too</em> and <em>as well</em> go at the end: <em>She plays tennis too. / She plays tennis as well.</em> They are for positive sentences. In a negative sentence, use <em>either</em> at the end: <em>I don't like it either.</em>",
        "tag": "also · too · either"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>She plays also tennis.</em> → ✓ <em>She also plays tennis.</em> (before the main verb) · ✗ <em>I don't like it too.</em> → ✓ <em>I don't like it either.</em> (negative sentence) · ✗ <em>Also Tom came.</em> → ✓ <em>Tom came too.</em> or <em>Tom also came.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "<em>Just</em> works like <em>only</em> when it means <em>nothing more</em>: <em>It's just a cold. I just want a cup of tea.</em> <em>Also</em> at the start of a sentence is only a linker, with a comma: <em>Also, we need milk.</em> For <em>not only … but also …</em>, see <a href=\"/paired-conjunctions\">Paired conjunctions</a>. For <em>nobody, nothing</em> and <em>no</em>, see <a href=\"/negative-words\">Negative words</a>. For the order of other adverbs, see <a href=\"/adverbs-of-frequency\">Adverbs of frequency</a>.",
